\description{
Validates a design specification, checks that the backend reproduces the
specified population (the check run), then computes power at a fixed n,
the sample size that reaches the target power, or the smallest
detectable effect.
}
\details{
\strong{Backends.} Basic tests run on pwr's exact formulas by default (Spower
simulation on request, and for Welch's t-test; two proportions on base
R's \code{stats::power.prop.test()} for equal groups and pwrss for unequal
groups), regression interactions on
InteractionPoweR, mediation and moderated mediation (PROCESS models 4, 6,
7 and 14) on power4mome, and 2x2 ANOVA designs on Superpower.
Wave 2 (engine only, not yet in the app): one-way between-participants
ANOVA on Superpower's \code{ANOVA_exact()}; multiple regression R-squared
(against 0, or an increase) on \code{pwrss::power.f.regression()}; one
proportion and McNemar's test on pwrss's exact tests
(\code{power.exact.oneprop()}, \code{power.exact.mcnemar()}); and Welch's t-test
by formula on request (\code{options > method = "exact"},
\code{pwrss::power.t.welch()}). These have no simulation option. For the two
exact tests of counts, whose power goes up and down with n, the required
n is the smallest that reaches the target power.
More tests by formula (engine only, not yet in the app), each on one
pwrss function: Fisher's exact test of two proportions
(\code{target_test > method = "fisher_exact"}, \code{power.exact.fisher()}), the
chi-square goodness-of-fit test (\code{power.chisq.gof()}, with Cohen's w from
\code{probs.to.w()}), two independent correlations (\code{power.z.twocors()}) and
one-way ANCOVA (\code{power.f.ancova.shieh()}). None has a simulation option;
Fisher's exact test is an exact test of counts too.
One-way repeated-measures ANOVA (template \code{oneway_within}): one
within-participants factor with three or more levels, the omnibus F test
on Superpower's \code{ANOVA_exact()} (design \code{"kw"}), with one SD and one
correlation \code{population > r} for every pair of conditions, so sphericity
holds. \code{goal > n} is the number of participants, each in every condition.
Mixed ANOVA beyond 2 x 2 (template \code{mixed}): one between factor A by one
within factor B, at least one with three or more levels (for example
group by time with three timepoints), on Superpower's \code{ANOVA_exact()}
(design \code{"kb*mw"}), with one SD and one correlation, so sphericity holds.
\code{population > means} has one row per level of A, one mean per level of B.
Rank tests (\code{target_test > method} \code{"wilcoxon_signed_rank"} for
\code{one_sample_t} and \code{paired_t}, \code{"mann_whitney"} for \code{independent_t},
\code{"kruskal_wallis"} for \code{oneway_between}): simulated with Spower's
\code{p_wilcox.test()} and \code{p_kruskal.test()} on scores of the shape in
\code{population > score_shape} (normal, skewed or heavy-tailed; the
signed-rank test, which assumes symmetry, normal or heavy-tailed),
shifted by the effect. No formula.
Two-way ANCOVA (template \code{factorial_ancova}): two between factors, either
main effect or the interaction, by pwrss's \code{power.f.ancova.shieh()}
(Shieh's method) with the effect's rows of \code{factorial.contrasts()};
adjusted cell means laid out as for \code{mixed}, equal cells.
Two-factor ANOVA with both factors between participants, beyond 2 x 2
(template \code{factorial_between}, design \code{"kb*mb"}), or both within
participants, 2 x 2 or larger (template \code{factorial_within}, design
\code{"kw*mw"}, one correlation between every pair of cells, so sphericity
holds): either main effect or the interaction on Superpower's
\code{ANOVA_exact()}, with means laid out as for \code{mixed}. \code{goal > n} is the
total number of participants (split equally across the cells for the
between design; each in every cell for the within design).
Bayes factor t-tests (\code{target_test > method} \code{"bayes_factor"} for
\code{one_sample_t}, \code{paired_t} and \code{independent_t}, equal groups): power is
the probability that the JZS Bayes factor BF10 reaches
\code{target_test > evidence_threshold} (default 10), by bfpwr's
\code{powertbf01()} in closed form, with a Cauchy analysis prior of scale
\code{target_test > prior_scale} (default 1/sqrt(2)) and a normal design
prior with SD \code{population > effect_sd} (default 0). No alpha.
Three-factor ANOVA (template \code{factorial_three}): factors A, B and C, each
between or within participants (between factors first), means as a
nested A by B by C array, one SD and one correlation between every pair
of a participant's cells; any main effect or interaction on
Superpower's \code{ANOVA_exact()}. \code{goal > n} is the total number of
participants, split equally across the between factors' combinations.
Two dependent correlations in one group (engine only), by Steiger's z
test with pwrss's \code{power.z.twocors.steiger()} (unpooled, as G*Power):
templates \code{overlapping_correlations} (X with Y against X with Z) and
\code{nonoverlapping_correlations} (X1 with Y1 against X2 with Y2).
Logistic regression (design \code{logistic_regression}, engine only): the Wald z
test of one predictor, a score (odds ratio per SD) or a yes/no group,
optionally adjusted for other predictors through their R-squared with
it, by pwrss's \code{power.z.logistic()} (Demidenko's method with variance
correction).
Poisson regression (design \code{poisson_regression}, engine only): the Wald z
test of one predictor of a count outcome, a score (rate ratio per SD) or
a yes/no group, over a mean exposure, optionally adjusted for other
predictors through their R-squared with it, by pwrss's
\code{power.z.poisson()} (Demidenko's method with variance correction).
Planned comparisons (design \code{factorial_anova}, template
\code{oneway_contrast}, engine only): the t test of a weighted combination of
one factor's group means, by pwrss's \code{power.t.contrasts()}. The weights
go in \code{target_test > weights}, one per level, adding up to 0; a trend
test is the contrast with the matching weights. Two-sided only, equal
groups, one contrast at a time.
Generic tests (design \code{generic_test}, engine only): power for a t, F,
chi-square or z statistic's noncentrality parameter, or the
noncentrality needed for a target power, with pwrss's generic functions.
They have no sample size; the result's curve has one row with \code{n} \code{NA}.
Cluster-randomised trials (design \code{cluster_trial}, template
\code{cluster_randomised}): whole groups are assigned to one of two
conditions and a score compared, by powertools' \code{crt.parallel.cont()}
(the design effect with the degrees of freedom from the number of
groups). \code{goal > n} is the total number of participants, a whole number
of groups of \code{population > cluster_size}, the same number of groups in
each condition. Multisite trials (template \code{multisite}): people are
randomised to the two conditions within each group, by powertools'
\code{multisite.cont()}; \code{goal > n} is a whole number of groups. Group
treatment trials (template \code{group_treatment}): people are randomised one
by one and the second condition is delivered in groups, by powertools'
\code{irgtt.cont()} or, for a yes/no outcome, \code{irgtt.bin()}; \code{goal > n} is
split equally between the conditions.
Structural equation models (design \code{sem}, template \code{model_fit}): the
power of the chi-square test of exact fit to reject a model whose misfit
in the population is \code{population > rmsea}, by semPower's
\code{semPower.postHoc()}. The model's degrees of freedom are
\code{population > df}, or are counted by \code{semPower.getDf()} for a
confirmatory factor analysis of \code{population > indicators} items on
\code{population > factors} correlated factors. Goals: power and sample size.
Three more templates test one restriction against the model without
it, by the chi-square difference (likelihood ratio) test: measurement
invariance of one factor across two groups (template \code{invariance},
metric or scalar, semPower's \code{semPower.powerMI()}; \code{goal > n} is split
between the groups by \code{options > allocation_ratio}), the correlation
between two factors against zero (template \code{factor_correlation},
\code{semPower.powerCFA()}) and one slope of a latent regression against
zero (template \code{latent_regression}, \code{semPower.powerRegression()}).

\strong{Method.} \code{options > method} chooses how power is computed. For 2x2
ANOVA designs the default is \code{"exact"} (Superpower's \code{ANOVA_exact()},
exact for the specified population), and for basic tests other than
Welch's t-test it is \code{"exact"} too (pwr's analytic formulas: noncentral
t and chi-square distributions; Fisher's z for a correlation, an
approximation). Two proportions use the z test with pooled variance under
the null and no continuity correction, as G*Power does
(\code{power.prop.test(strict = TRUE)} for equal groups,
\code{pwrss::power.z.twoprops()} for unequal groups); with
\code{options > proportions_formula = "arcsine"} they use pwr's Cohen's h (the
arcsine approximation). \code{"simulation"} uses Monte Carlo
simulation with \code{options > nrep} replications (default 1,000) and
\code{options > seed} (drawn and recorded if not given). Every other design
is simulated. Set
\code{options(powerkit.cores = k)} or the environment variable \code{POWERKIT_CORES}
to spread simulation replications over \code{k} cores (Unix-alikes only).
Mediation and moderated-mediation simulations run in batches of at most
200 replications per power4mome call (fewer above 1,000 draws per
confidence interval), keeping only each batch's counts, so memory use
doesn't grow with \code{options > nrep}.

\strong{Sensitivity.} For 2x2 designs, the shape of the specified pattern is
kept and its size scaled (each cell mean's deviation from the grand mean)
until the target effect reaches the target power at n. Sensitivity is not
yet available for other designs (the one-way ANOVA included).
}
